# Structural optimization applied:
#   The original kernel used one thread per output element with a flat
#   grid-stride loop over (N, Cout, Dout, Hout, Wout).  Every thread
#   independently re-read the *entire* weight slice for its output channel
#   from global memory, even though all threads that share the same (n, oc)
#   pair (which, for these problem sizes, is essentially every thread inside
#   a CTA since the spatial extent Dout*Hout*Wout is vastly larger than the
#   block size) use the *exact same* weight values.
#
#   This version restructures the launch as a 2D grid:
#     - blockIdx.y enumerates the (n, oc) pairs directly (grid.y = N*Cout).
#     - blockIdx.x together with a grid-stride loop tiles the spatial output
#       positions (Dout*Hout*Wout) for that fixed (n, oc).
#   Each CTA cooperatively loads the small weight slice
#   (Cin_per_group * kD * kH * kW elements) needed for its output channel
#   into dynamic shared memory exactly once, synchronizes, and then reuses
#   that cached weight tile from shared memory for every spatial position it
#   computes, instead of re-fetching it from global memory per thread.
